{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py resolve paper:din\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --find 'normalization' --lines 38\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve unirank-benchmark-v2\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-benchmark-v2 --start-line 1 --lines 55\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve unirank-code:UniMixer\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-code:UniMixer --start-line 175 --lines 30\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"title\": \"Deep Interest Network for Click-Through Rate Prediction\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"status\": \"abstract_read; inspect full mechanism and author implementation before a detailed reproduction claim\",\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"cached_unix\": 1793799695.3575318,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"retrieved_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://paper:din\",\n  \"text_lines\": 831\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 271,\n  \"next_line\": 309,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\\\_goods\\\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n292: Take ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\\n293: Only parameters of non-zero sparse features appearing in each mini-batch needs to be updated in the scenario of SGD based optimization methods without regularization.\\n294: However, when adding ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization it needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters for each mini-batch, which leads to extremely heavy computations and is unacceptable with parameters scaling up to hundreds of millions.\\n295: Figure 3. Control function of PReLU and Dice.\\n296: In this paper, we introduce an efficient mini-batch aware regularizer, which only calculates the L2-norm over the parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch and makes the computation possible.\\n297: In fact, it is the embedding dictionary that contributes most of the parameters for CTR networks and arises the difficulty of heavy computation.\\n298: Let 𝐖∈ℝD×K\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K} denote parameters of the whole embedding dictionary, with DD as the dimensionality of the embedding vector and KK as the dimensionality of feature space.\\n299: Expand the ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization on 𝐖\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}} over samples\\n300: (4)\\n301: L2​(𝐖)=‖𝐖‖22=∑j=1K‖𝒘j‖22=∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮∑j=1KI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})&=\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\\\end{split}\\n302: where 𝒘j∈ℝD\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D} is the jj-th embedding vector,\\n303: I⁡(𝒙j≠0)I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0) denotes if the instance 𝒙\\\\bm{x} has the feature id jj, and njn_{j} denotes the number of occurrence for feature id jj in all samples. Eq.(4) can be transformed into Eq.(5) in the mini-batch aware manner\\n304: (5)\\n305: L2​(𝐖)=∑j=1K∑m=1B∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬmI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\n306: where BB denotes the number of mini-batches, ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m} denotes the mm-th mini-batch.\\n307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"title\": \"UniRank: Benchmarking Ranking Models for Unified Sequential Modeling and Feature Interaction\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"kind\": \"benchmark_paper\",\n  \"content_read\": true,\n  \"read_scope\": \"Section2.3 representative models; protocol and efficiency checked separately during adapter setup.\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"retrieved_on\": \"2026-11-06\",\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"a5a9d5475c2e09fc89caa6a9b4b5d2da0f48aef28fea78549278f6388ebfb5d4\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"text_lines\": 39\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"total_lines\": 39,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 40,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\\n2: Version: arxiv:2607.19987v2\\n3: Scope: section2.3 only, original benchmark method descriptions.\\n4: \\n5: 2.3. Representative Models\\n6: 2.3.1. Stacked Unified Interaction\\n7: This paradigm arranges sequential modeling and feature interaction as consecutive modules. The sequence module first encodes the behavioral history, and the feature interaction module then combines this representation with user, target-item, and context features.\\n8: •\\n9: HiFormer (Gui et al., 2023) pools the behavior history with target attention, tokenizes heterogeneous fields and task queries, and applies hierarchical HiFormer blocks to the stacked tokens.\\n10: •\\n11: RankMixer (Zhu et al., 2025) pools the history with target attention, tokenizes concatenated ranking fields into chunks, and alternates parameter-free Token Mixing with per-token MLPs.\\n12: •\\n13: Zenith (Zhang et al., 2026) separately embeds user and item identifiers, pools historical item attributes with target attention, tokenizes context and target attributes, and applies Zenith interaction blocks.\\n14: •\\n15: TokenMixer (Jiang et al., 2026) combines target-aware sequence summaries with global and group tokens, then applies reversible token mixing and per-token feed-forward networks.\\n16: •\\n17: UniMixer (Ha et al., 2026) flattens histories with user and context fields, then chunk-tokenizes them for UniMixing-Lite blocks.\\n18: •\\n19: HeMix (Wang et al., 2026) combines fixed and feature-conditioned queries with low-rank token mixing over recent and earlier histories.\\n20: •\\n21: SSR (Yu et al., 2026) uses target-attention sequence pooling and multi-view competitive sparse filters with dense fusion.\\n22: 2.3.2. Layer-wise Unified Interaction\\n23: This paradigm integrates sequential and non-sequential representations throughout every network layer.\\n24: •\\n25: OneTrans (Zhang et al., 2025) injects sequence and partitioned non-sequential tokens into every block, using mixed attention, a shared sequence FFN, and token-specific FFNs.\\n26: •\\n27: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026b) encodes the sequence per layer, decodes global queries, and enriches them with non-sequential tokens.\\n28: •\\n29: MixFormer (Huang et al., 2026a) mixes non-sequential queries, uses cross-attention to retrieve information from the updated sequence, and fuses the resulting representations.\\n30: •\\n31: INFNet (Li et al., 2025b) organizes categorical, sequential, and task tokens around a hub for layer-wise aggregation and broadcasting.\\n32: •\\n33: EST (Liu et al., 2026) uses cross-attention to sequence representations and feature-specific feed-forward layers.\\n34: •\\n35: TokenFormer (Zhou et al., 2026) represents fields, histories, and targets as tokens with full interaction and sliding-window Flex Attention.\\n36: •\\n37: LONGER (Chai et al., 2025) encodes local groups with inner Transformers and joins compressed long-term history with recent queries.\\n38: •\\n39: UltraHSTU (Ding et al., 2026) extends HSTU-style sequential transduction (Zhai et al., 2024) with hybrid attention that combines sparse and full attention in each interaction layer.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:UniMixer\",\n  \"title\": \"UniMixer benchmark implementation\",\n  \"kind\": \"benchmark_reimplementation\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/UniMixer.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"content_read\": true,\n  \"read_scope\": \"Representative forward paths and structural definitions; no end-to-end reproduction of this method.\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"locators\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"UniMixer.forward\",\n      \"line\": 175,\n      \"end_line\": 200\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"UniMixingLiteBlocks.forward\",\n      \"line\": 305,\n      \"end_line\": 315\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"UniMixingLiteLayer.forward\",\n      \"line\": 436,\n      \"end_line\": 437\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"SiameseFeedForwardLayer.forward\",\n      \"line\": 466,\n      \"end_line\": 467\n    }\n  ],\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"3d9ba455a8b765bc778f1fa0dcd00d07680a0d9019e792371ef5346d90dd3e31\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"3d9ba455a8b765bc778f1fa0dcd00d07680a0d9019e792371ef5346d90dd3e31\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://unirank-code:UniMixer\",\n  \"text_lines\": 467\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:UniMixer\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/UniMixer.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"3d9ba455a8b765bc778f1fa0dcd00d07680a0d9019e792371ef5346d90dd3e31\",\n  \"total_lines\": 467,\n  \"start_line\": 175,\n  \"next_line\": 205,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"175:     def forward(self, inputs):\\n176:         batch_dict, item_dict, mask = self.get_inputs(inputs)\\n177:         batch_size = mask.shape[0]\\n178:         item_seq_emb = self.embedding_layer(item_dict, flatten_emb=True)\\n179:         item_seq_emb = item_seq_emb.view(batch_size, -1)\\n180: \\n181:         user_context_emb = self.embedding_layer(batch_dict, flatten_emb=True)\\n182: \\n183:         feature_embeddings = torch.cat([user_context_emb, item_seq_emb], dim=-1)\\n184: \\n185:         unified_tokens = self.unified_tokenizer_layer(feature_embeddings)\\n186: \\n187:         unified_tokens = self.activation_checkpoint(\\n188:             self.unified_interaction_layers,\\n189:             unified_tokens\\n190:         )\\n191: \\n192:         bottom_output = unified_tokens.flatten(start_dim=1)\\n193:         tower_output = [self.tower[i](bottom_output) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n194:         y_pred = [self.output_activation[i](tower_output[i]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n195: \\n196:         return_dict = {}\\n197:         labels = self.feature_map.labels\\n198:         for i in range(self.num_tasks):\\n199:             return_dict[f\\\"{labels[i]}_pred\\\"] = y_pred[i]\\n200:         return return_dict\\n201: \\n202: \\n203: class UniMixingLiteBlocks(nn.Module):\\n204:     def __init__(self,\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
